+\section{Simulation Design Best Practices}
+Radar simulation is useful only when the claim boundary is explicit. EchoForge follows four practices that a radar reviewer should expect. First, it separates the public-proxy target model from detector-facing features: airframe geometry, speed, RCS/aspect, phase, and clutter parameters are generator-side assumptions, while the model sees only derived detector-view summaries. Second, it separates scenario grouping from phase rows: take-up, climb, and cruise records from one scenario group inherit the same split assignment. Third, it records every sensor branch as a role-compatible public envelope rather than a fielded-sensor replica. Fourth, it scores the blind holdout once, after train/CV thresholds, calibrators, and EI selection locks are written.
+
+\subsection{Monte Carlo Scenario Construction}
+The Monte Carlo run samples public-proxy positives and hard negatives across site archetype, range band, target aspect, noise/clutter regime, and phase. The three phase windows are not cosmetic labels. Initial take-up is deliberately hardest because near-ground coupling, rail/catapult launch proxy, transient kinematics, and multipath are strongest in the first 0--30~s. Climb transition keeps changing geometry and increasing separation over 30--90~s. Cruise altitude is the most settled 90--150~s interval and should therefore be the easiest phase if the generator and detectors are behaving sensibly. This is why the phase figure is a sanity check as much as a performance chart.
+
+\subsection{Positive Public Proxy and Hard Negatives}
+The fixed-wing pusher-prop public proxy uses open-source geometry and kinematic envelopes for an Iranian-drone-like fixed-wing pusher family, but it does not import measured radar truth. The appendix maps this proxy to length/span/height, speed, launch/take-up, RCS/aspect, and pusher-prop micro-Doppler bands. Birds and RC aircraft are modeled as hard negatives because they stress the same low-altitude radar observables: small body returns, wingbeat or prop cadence, coastal clutter, and track fragmentation. The generator therefore asks a narrow question: under these declared public priors, can a detector keep low-FPR recall high without overreacting to benign aerial clutter?
+
+\subsection{Noise, Clutter, RFI, and Receiver Effects}
+The environment is not treated as white noise only. The evidence bundle records thermal/SNR and CNR stress buckets, Weibull/K-like clutter families, sea/weather/terrain glint, vegetation motion, multipath ghosts, RFI bursts, passive-RF missingness, AGC compression, clock drift, quantization, dropped CPIs, Doppler folding, and calibration offset. These are synthetic stressors rather than site-measured truth. Their purpose is to prevent a clean-signal benchmark and to expose failure modes in branch detectors and fusion logic.
+
+\subsection{Raw Data Processing Best Practices}
+The recommended processing order is conventional: form range-Doppler summaries, apply CFAR/MTD-style detection features, compute micro-Doppler concentration and spread, construct track/cue confidence, calibrate scores on train/CV rows, and evaluate rare-positive behavior with precision-recall and low-FPR operating metrics. EchoForge follows standard radar detection and classifier-calibration practice by reporting ROC AUC, AP, selected-threshold counts, Recall@$\leq$1\%FPR, Brier score, ECE, and group-block bootstrap uncertainty rather than relying on a single leaderboard number \cite{rohling1983,richards2014,kay1998,fawcett2006,davis2006,saito2015,platt1999,niculescu2005}.
+
+\section{Detector Processing and Sensor Fusion}
+The detector branches are best read as progressively stronger processing lanes. First, the individual radar and cue branches test whether a single observable family carries useful signal. Second, tabular and sequence ML baselines test ordinary learned processing on detector-facing features. Third, accepted human-engineered fusion combines source confidence, stale-track penalties, and cross-sensor confirmation. Finally, EI searches train/CV detector-view scores for sparse calibrated fusion components and freezes the selected solution before holdout scoring.
